---
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import { loadFeed } from '../lib/feed.ts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'; import { COMMON } from '../lib/methodology.ts'; import { RATES, CHARGES_VERSION, chargesFor, sum } from '../lib/charges.ts';  import { ledger } from '../lib/data.ts'; import { dmy } from '../lib/view.ts'; import { GLOSSARY, KINDS } from '../lib/glossary.ts';
const fees = sum(chargesFor("buy", 100, 100)) + sum(chargesFor("sell", 50, 120));
const L = ledger();
import archive from '../../ledger/snapshots/2026-11-17.json';
const run = archive.runs.find(r => r.screen_id === 'conservative-quality')!;
const fill = archive.picks.find(p => p.screen_id === run.screen_id && p.symbol === 'CAMS')!.fills[0];
const miss = run.near_miss.find(m => m.symbol === 'ABBOTINDIA')!;
 const F = loadFeed(); const weights = F.meta.weights ?? {};
---
<Base title="Method" description="How every figure on Rulebook is made: the ledger, the accounting, how calls are chosen, the data sources, every number defined once, and what changed and why.">
<div class="wrap prose reader-intro"><PageIntro title="Method" summary="Inspect the rules, accounting assumptions and source records behind the figures.">Method</PageIntro></div>
<div class="wrap prose">
<nav aria-label="Method contents"><a href="#accounting">Accounting</a> · <a href="#price-basis">Price basis</a> · <a href="#reproduce">Reproduce a record</a> · <a href="#numbers">Glossary</a> · <a href="#changes">Version history</a></nav><p>Paper fills use closing prices. Slippage, partial fills and taxes on gains can change a real outcome. A short record cannot establish skill.</p><h2 data-u="mt6" id="accounting">The method, in short</h2>
<details class="table-alt" open><summary>One ledger, append-only</summary><p>A single record, rebuilt each trading night, holds the screens, every weekly run, every call, trade and void, and one book snapshot a day. New nights add rows and never rewrite old ones; a test fails the build if they try. Pages format it and never compute their own version. As of {dmy(L.as_of)}.</p></details>
<details class="table-alt"><summary>The accounting, with a worked example</summary><ul><li><strong>Realised</strong> is what exits have booked, partial exits included, after their share of charges.</li><li><strong>Unrealised</strong> is the remaining quantity against its remaining cost, after the buy charges that belong to it.</li><li><strong>Every charge is counted once</strong>, attached to one fill.</li><li><strong>R</strong> is measured against the original risk to that call's own stop, set at entry and unchanged by partial exits.</li></ul><p>Before charges: buy 100 at ₹100 with a stop at ₹90, sell 50 at ₹120, mark the other 50 at ₹110. Realised ₹1,000, unrealised ₹500, total ₹1,500, initial risk ₹1,000, so 1.5R. With schedule {CHARGES_VERSION}, those fills incur ₹{fees.toFixed(2)} in charges. Net P&amp;L is ₹{(1500-fees).toFixed(2)}, or {((1500-fees)/1000).toFixed(3)}R on the same initial risk. As a second example, buying and selling at the same price produces zero gross P&amp;L and a net loss equal to both fills’ charges.</p></details>
<details class="table-alt"><summary>How calls are chosen</summary><p>{COMMON.book}</p><p>{COMMON.mechanics}</p><p>{COMMON.size}</p></details>
<details class="table-alt"><summary>What the simulation leaves out</summary><p>{COMMON.basis}</p><p>Real trades have slippage, partial fills, taxes on gains and halts that this simulation ignores or simplifies, which makes the simulated record more flattering than a real one would be.</p><p>{COMMON.lag}</p><p>{COMMON.missing}</p></details>
<details class="table-alt"><summary>Charges and benchmark</summary><p>Schedule {CHARGES_VERSION}: delivery equity. Securities transaction tax {RATES.sttPct}% on both sides, exchange transaction charge {RATES.exchangePct}%, SEBI fee {RATES.sebiPct}%, stamp duty {RATES.stampBuyPct}% on buys, GST {RATES.gstPct}% on exchange and SEBI fees, and a flat ₹{RATES.dpSellFlat} depository charge on sells. These are simulation assumptions. <a href="https://www.nseindia.com/static/invest/first-time-investor-sebi-turnover-fees-stt-other-levies">NSE’s statutory levies table</a> lists delivery STT of 0.1% on each side under the schedule effective 1 April 2026, buyer stamp duty of 0.015% from 1 July 2020, the SEBI turnover fee and 18% GST on brokerage services. The model’s exchange rate differs from <a href="https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/circulars/FA73061.pdf">NSE circular FA73061</a>: effective 1 March 2026, the cash-market charge was ₹306.99 per crore on each side, plus ₹0.01 IPFT. This model uses ₹297 and omits IPFT, so it understates this component by ₹10 per crore per side. Recorded fills retain their original assumptions. The depository fee varies by broker. The benchmark is the Nifty 500 price index, which excludes dividends; the simulated calls exclude them too, so the two are compared on the same basis.</p></details>
<details class="table-alt"><summary>Sample size</summary><p>Every statistic shows the number of closed trades behind it. Under 30, it carries the label "too few to conclude anything". The record is new and takes months to reach that size.</p></details>
<h2 data-u="mt6" id="price-basis">How to read the numbers</h2>
<p>Daily-feed figures and the public record use dated closing prices. Delayed quotes can come from a later session: their timestamp is the exchange print time, not the time you opened this page. The Last price and P&amp;L in a call row use the same quote. A missing quote leaves that row at its labelled ledger close.</p>
<p>P&amp;L before charges is quantity × (last price − entry price). After charges also deducts the costs attached to the fills. Cash is the starting capital plus sell proceeds, minus buy costs and all charges. The record's return is cumulative since inception, not a daily return; drawdown includes entry charges from the initial zero-return baseline. If the benchmark's starting close is missing, its return stays missing.</p>
<p>Market pulse, regime and barometer are separate dated sources. Values from different sessions are not directly comparable. Peer counts include only companies with the particular metric, so their denominators can differ.</p>
<h2 data-u="mt6">Data</h2><p>{COMMON.sources}</p>
<h2 id="reproduce">Reproduce a record</h2>
<p><a href="/data/snapshot.json">Download the dated snapshot definitions and counts</a>. To check 200-day breadth, count daily screen rows where the closing price exceeds a positive 200-day moving average. Divide by rows with both inputs. Daily direction uses the sign of the published daily percentage change, including zero as unchanged.</p>
<h3>17 November 2026 · Conservative Quality</h3><p>The archived run records {run.passed.length} passes, {run.listed.length} listed stocks and {run.taken.length} entries, from {run.universe_n} companies considered ({run.not_scorable_n} without sufficient inputs). CAMS was number {run.listed.indexOf('CAMS') + 1} on the list. Its ₹{archive.config.ticket.toLocaleString('en-IN')} ticket divided by the ₹{fill.price} entry close gave {fill.qty} whole shares, or ₹{(fill.price * fill.qty).toLocaleString('en-IN')} before charges.</p><p>{miss.symbol} missed one rule: {miss.failed_rule.toLowerCase()} ({miss.gap}). INFY passed and was listed, but the book had already allocated it to Magic Formula. The archive records outcomes and list order; the underlying composite scores and full historical inputs were not stored.</p><p><a href="/data/screen-run-2026-11-17.json">Inspect the dated run, exclusions and original fills (JSON)</a>.</p>
<h2 data-u="mt6" id="numbers">Every number, defined once</h2><p>Each figure on the site is one of three kinds. <strong>Fact</strong> is {KINDS.Fact}. <strong>Model</strong> is {KINDS.Model}. <strong>Result</strong> is {KINDS.Result}. Rulebook publishes no opinions. If a figure on a page disagrees with this list, this list is the one to trust, and please tell us.</p>
<p class="src">Composite weights from the feed dated {dmy(F.asOf)}: {Object.entries(weights).map(([k, v]) => `${k} ${typeof v === "number" ? Math.round(v * 100) + "%" : "missing"}`).join(" · ") || "not supplied"}. We reproduce the feed’s score; these weights alone do not verify the inputs or every component’s calculation.</p><div class="gloss">{GLOSSARY.map((g) => <details id={g.term.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g,"-").replace(/-$/,"")} class="table-alt"><summary><span class="kind" data-kind={g.kind}>{g.kind}</span> {g.term}</summary><p>{g.what}</p><p><strong>How it is computed.</strong> {g.how}</p><p><strong>Wrong if.</strong> {g.wrongIf}</p></details>)}</div>
<h2 data-u="mt6" id="changes">Published method notes</h2><p>The current paper book uses version 2 screens, first run on 17 November 2026. Each screen lists up to five names; a listed stock is not necessarily taken. The Calls page carries each run's date and exit rules. The <a href="https://github.com/caakshayk1-boop/alpha/tree/main/apps/lockedfree/src/lib">published implementation</a> defines screen thresholds, missing-input exclusions, ranking and allocation. Magic Formula ranks eligible rows by the source rank. Other lists rank by composite score, with symbol order breaking ties. The source provides Piotroski pass bits and precomputed component scores. Underlying filing inputs for each bit and every score transformation are unavailable here.</p>
</div></Base>
